 Of the 4,530 completed robot actions in REASSEMBLE, 509 ended in failure. A model that predicted success every time would therefore be correct on almost 89\% of the actions. It would also miss every failure. For this study, a more useful question was whether the model gave higher failure scores to the actions that actually failed. To test what the camera added, the camera–sensor model and the sensor-only model had to predict the outcomes of the same actions, with the same recordings held out for assessment.
 The PHM gear study needed a different test. There, the model predicted a number derived from a photograph taken after a gear run. Its error could be measured in percentage points against that photographic target. The same error could not tell us how closely the prediction matched physical tooth damage, because the study had no independent measurement of that damage.
 These two cases explain the main design choice in this thesis. Each comparison starts with the physical event, the target attached to it, and the information available when the model makes its prediction. The choice of data split and performance measure follows from those decisions.
-\section{Research design and research questions} \label{sec:ch4-design}
-REASSEMBLE was the main study of multimodal fusion. Its hand camera and machine sensors recorded the same robotic actions, and each completed action had a recorded outcome. This made it possible to ask three questions on the same cases. How well could the camera predict failure on its own? How well could the sensors predict it? Did combining the two improve on the stronger individual predictor?
-The other studies tested related questions under different conditions. In PHM, the model combined groups of machine measurements to predict a gear-condition indicator. In MSM, the question was whether video added information about abnormal cutting beyond what the milling machine’s own measurements already provided. ViFailback tested complete manipulation episodes on held-out tasks. It also examined whether visual features learned on REASSEMBLE remained useful for a different robot dataset. HANDOVER supplied video and force–torque measurements from human–robot trials. Its official test split supported a comparison of the two individual predictors and their fixed average.
-The five research requirements guided how these tests were read. \textbf{R1} concerned the target and the pairing of observations. \textbf{R2} concerned how each input was represented. \textbf{R3} asked what fusion added over a strong single input. \textbf{R4} concerned the response to missing or damaged inputs. \textbf{R5} concerned the strength of the evidence, including the choice of test groups, the uncertainty of the results, and the cost of the method.
+\section{Research design and research questions} \label{sec:ch4-design} \label{sec:literature-study-gaps}
+The welding studies in Chapter~\ref{ch:literature} give different answers about the value of a second input. Figure~\ref{fig:fusion-research-gap} shows how those comparisons led to five questions for reading the empirical evidence. The requirements provide a common framework for the studies as they developed.
+\begin{figure}[H] \centering \caption{From welding evidence to requirements for testing fusion} \label{fig:ch02-synthesis-requirements} \label{fig:fusion-research-gap} \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/fusion_research_gap.pdf} \displaynote{DMAW reported 96.1\% accuracy for fusion and 90.2\% for its strongest single input \parencite{fan_dmaw_2026}. In weld penetration, both single-input models and fusion reached 100\% on the clean test set \parencite{hua_anti-interference_2026}. The reported contrasts are within each study.} \end{figure}
+\textbf{R1. What is predicted, and when?} A camera can supply a predictor or a reference measured after the event. For each study, we identify the physical event, when each observation was available, and how the target was obtained. In PHM, post-run photographs supply a proxy for gear condition. In REASSEMBLE, the camera and sensors are predictors paired with a recorded action outcome.
+\textbf{R2. What does each representation retain (SQ1)?} Published results for engineered features, pretrained visual features and temporal patches concern different targets and fitting conditions \parencite{horwitz_back_2023,roth_towards_2022,nie_time_2023}. We compare individual branches on the same target and observation window, recording their training histories and fitting budgets. A branch's standalone score leaves open whether it contributes information to another predictor.
+\textbf{R3. What does fusion add (SQ2)?} The clean welding tie and the loss after adding gripper state in handover make the strongest single input an essential comparator \parencite{hua_anti-interference_2026,thoduka_multimodal_2024}. On the 4,530 eligible REASSEMBLE actions, we compare camera and sensor predictions, fixed averaging and fitted combinations with the same recordings held out. The feature-level model F3 projects a 128-dimensional visual representation and 220 sensor statistics to two 128-dimensional vectors and concatenates them. This is distinct from combining two representations of the sensor stream. Chapter~\ref{ch:reassemble} gives the fitting and calibration differences that limit attribution to the fusion rule alone.
+\textbf{R4. Which reliability condition is being tested (SQ3)?} Knowing an input is absent calls for a different response from receiving a degraded but apparently available signal \parencite{deng_tc-maf_2026,zhang_provable_2023}. We identify the affected input and what the model knows about its condition. In the matched REASSEMBLE comparison, static and adaptive models receive comparable corruption exposure during fitting. Both are assessed on clean and corrupted cases. Timing changes and transfer to another domain are separate tests.
+\textbf{R5. How dependable is the comparison (SQ4)?} Selection on related observations can inflate performance estimates \parencite{varma_bias_2006,kapoor_leakage_2023}. The assessment unit follows the acquisition process, such as a gear experiment, recording, or manipulation task. Ranking, probability calibration and uncertainty answer different questions about failure prediction. Across datasets, we distinguish refitting a procedure, transferring representation weights and testing an unchanged predictor. Table~\ref{tab:fusion-study-scope} identifies which of these claims the available data support.
